# Data Cleaning Comparison (Fatima vs. Misa)

- Compared two independently cleaned datasets using AI (Claude Haiku) to score cleanliness
- Perceived MH Annual (Fatima): scored 90%, cleaner format, selected for use
- Catalog Entry Mental Health Characteristics (Misa): 8,000 rows vs. Fatima’s 11,000, different starting data
- Decision: use Fatima’s cleaned files for both datasets
- Quality flags columns to be removed across all files (API artifact, not needed for analysis)

# EDA Setup and Workflow

- Loaded all cleaned CSVs into Jupyter Notebook, created DataFrames per file
- Approach: use Claude (Claude.ai project with file access) to run EDA per file, not raw chat
- EDA steps per file: column inventory, descriptive stats, group-by aggregations, CI validation, visualizations
- CI validation checks added:
  - CI low < value < CI high
  - CI low > 0, CI high < 100
  - CI width column added (CI high minus CI low)
- Decision: create a separate notebook file per CSV for organization, merge into one report later

# Key EDA Findings: Mental Health Trends

- Every measured disorder rose between 2019 and 2024, none fell
- Fair/poor self-rated mental health rose ~6 percentage points; excellent/very good fell from 63% to 50%
- Self-reported anxiety disorder nearly doubled over the window
- Cannabis use: highest single 12-month indicator at 22%, biggest mover (+8.9 pp), plausibly tied to 2018 legalization
- Suicidal thoughts prevalence (2015–2019): essentially flat at ~12%, within confidence interval
  - Consultation with professionals rose more than ideation itself, suggesting increased help-seeking
- General perceived health (quarterly, 2021–2023): fell steadily, excellent/very good dropped from 57% to 48%

# Sex Differences Across Datasets

- Women report higher rates of anxiety (22.4% vs. 11%), mood disorders, ideation, and help-seeking
- Consultation-to-ideation ratio for women: ~1.5x vs. men
- Men report higher rates of cannabis use, heavy drinking, schizophrenia, and disruptive disorders
- Key cross-source insight: women report more distress and use more services across 4 independent tables, but men die by suicide at 3x the rate
  - Flagged as a genuine multi-source insight, recommended for its own dashboard page

# Age and Vulnerable Groups

- 18–34 is the clearest high-risk group across anxiety, mood disorders, and ideation
- Both anxiety and mood disorders highest in youngest groups, declining steadily with age
- Stress sources (2019): schoolwork (24%) and work (20%) dominate, followed by financial concerns
  - Stress source data only asked of younger subset (12–34), not directly comparable to full-population indicators
- Coping ability improved slightly 2016–2019; sex differences in coping are small relative to age effect

# Regional Findings

- Quebec is a striking outlier: fair/poor mental health reporting at ~8.9%, roughly half of other provinces
  - Other provinces cluster between 15–17%; gap likely a cultural/reporting style effect, not a true health gap
- Nova Scotia: highest fair/poor self-rated mental health rate
- Territories and small provinces show visibly wider CI widths due to smaller sample sizes

# Emergency Department and CIHI Data Issues

- ED visit rates (youth, all diagnoses) dropped sharply in 2020–2021 during COVID lockdowns, then spiked post-2021
  - Falling ED rate flagged as potentially indicating reduced access, not improved health
- CIHI MH Services and Child Youth Services files mix percentage and unit-based indicators in the same column
  - Must split by units before drawing any combined chart; current visualizations for these files are unreliable
- Excel date corruption bug found in raw file: 9 age-range rows show values like “December” instead of age ranges
  - Bug is in the raw Stats Canada file, not introduced by team cleaning

